{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"### The strategy\n\nWe can first rewrite each rational term in the expression as a negative power of $x$.\n\nThen, the derivatives of these terms can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is negative.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Rewriting rational terms as negative powers\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{3}{x^6}-\\dfrac{1}{x^4}+5\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3x^{-6}-x^{-4}+5\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"### Differentiating using the power rule\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}\\dfrac{d}{dx}(3x^{-6}-x^{-4}+5)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-6})-\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^{-4})+\\dfrac{d}{dx}(5)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=3 (-6x^{-7})-(-4)x^{-5}+0\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-18x^{-7}+4x^{-5}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=-\\dfrac{18}{x^7}+\\dfrac{4}{x^5}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x^6}-\\dfrac{1}{x^4}+5\\right)=-\\dfrac{18}{x^7}+\\dfrac{4}{x^5}$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"$\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{x^6}-\\dfrac{1}{x^4}+5\\right)=$ [[☃ expression 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"expression 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answerForms":[{"considered":"correct","form":false,"key":0,"simplify":false,"value":"-\\frac{18}{x^7}+\\frac{4}{x^5}"}],"buttonSets":["basic","prealgebra"],"functions":["f","g","h"],"times":false},"static":false,"type":"expression","version":{"major":1,"minor":0}}}}}
